window.TLT_INDEX = [
  {"p":"circuit-to-line.html","t":"When a circuit becomes a line","n":"02","l":"Kirchhoff's laws are not laws. They are an approximation with a stated domain of validity, and that domain has a sharp and computable edge. Finding the edge tells you exactly when a piece of wire stops being a connection and starts being a component.","s":[{"h":"The assumption nobody states","i":"assumption","x":"Kirchhoff's voltage law says the potentials around a loop sum to zero. Kirchhoff's current law says the currents into a node sum to zero. Both are used as though they hold at every instant - and buried in that word instant is the whole problem. If you change the voltage at one end of a wire, when does the other end find out? Not immediately. The change propagates at the phase velocity of the medium, so it arrives after a delay Lumped circuit theory is what you get by declaring . Every node has one voltage, shared everywhere along it, at every moment. That is not a claim about wires being perfect; it is a claim ab"},{"h":"Electrical length","i":"electrical","x":"Compare the delay with the period of the signal and something clean happens. The delay is , the period is , and their ratio is The physical length and the velocity both cancel out. What survives is a single dimensionless number: how many wavelengths long the thing is . That number is the electrical length, and it is usually quoted as a phase: From here on, lengths are quoted in wavelengths almost without exception. A \"quarter-wave transformer\" is not 3 cm long; it is long, and what that is in centimetres depends on the frequency and the dielectric. Drag the electrical length below and watch the voltage profile al"},{"h":"How much error, exactly","i":"howmuch","x":"The two ends carry and . Their difference is itself a sinusoid, and a standard identity gives its amplitude: Notice that the familiar rule of thumb already tolerates a 62 % discrepancy between the ends of a supposedly equipotential node. It is a rule for rough work, not a guarantee. And notice there is no threshold anywhere in that table - nothing switches off, no regime changes. The error grows smoothly from nothing, and where you draw the line depends entirely on what you can tolerate."},{"h":"The way out","i":"fix","x":"If the failure is that a single lumped element cannot represent something whose voltage varies along its length, there is an obvious repair: use more elements . Chop the line into short pieces, each short enough for lumped theory to hold inside it, and chain them. Each little piece needs four things to represent what a real pair of conductors does: The primes mark these as per unit length - ohms per metre, henries per metre, and so on - because a piece twice as long has twice as much of each. That single convention is what makes the model work for a line of any length. Then take the pieces to be infinitesimally s"}]},
  {"p":"index.html","t":"Transmission Line Theory","n":"","l":"Eleven chapters that build transmission-line theory in one continuous argument. A length of cable stops behaving like a wire and starts behaving like a medium; from that single fact come reflection, standing waves, impedance transformation and every chart and ","s":[{"h":"Tools","i":"","x":"For working problems rather than reading through."},{"h":"What this is","i":"","x":"Transmission lines are often taught as a set of rules handed down: here is a reflection coefficient, here is a standing wave ratio, here is a chart covered in circles. Learned that way the formulas are arbitrary and the factors of two feel like something to memorise rather than something to understand. They are not arbitrary. Each one follows from the one before it, and the chain is short enough to hold in your head end to end. Each chapter runs on two tracks . The main narrative is written to be read straight through without heavy mathematics. Wherever a step needs vector calculus or a long algebraic grind, that"},{"h":"Prologue","i":"","x":"Where the transmission-line model comes from in the first place. Optional. Chapter 01 onwards is self-contained and assumes none of this - but if you have ever wondered why a cable should be modelled as a ladder of inductors and capacitors, rather than simply accepting that it is, the answer is here."},{"h":"Contents","i":"","x":""},{"h":"How to use it","i":"","x":"Wherever a formula has a parameter, the figure beside it has a control for that parameter. Drag it. Distances are in wavelengths throughout, so nothing depends on a particular frequency, and lines are lossless unless a chapter says otherwise. The chapters are in order, but each one names what it assumes at the top, so you can start in the middle and reach back for what is missing."}]},
  {"p":"input-impedance.html","t":"Input impedance","n":"08","l":"A generator driving a line does not see the load. It sees the load through the line, and what arrives at its terminals depends on how far away the load is. This is the chapter where a length of cable stops being a connection and becomes a component in its own ","s":[{"h":"What the generator sees","i":"setup","x":"Chapter 04 warned that on a line carrying two waves, the ratio of total voltage to total current is not . Now we work out what it actually is. Standing a distance back from the load: Their ratio is the input impedance at that point: whatever a source connected there would measure. That form is worth pausing on, because it says something structural. The relationship between and impedance is always the same function . What changes along the line is not the rule, only the value of being fed into it - and chapter 06 already told us how that value moves: same magnitude, angle turning at ."},{"h":"The standard form","i":"tan","x":"Substituting and grinding through the exponentials gives the expression you will actually use: Everything you need follows by putting particular values into that one expression. The first row deserves emphasis because it is the goal of all matching work. When there is no reflected wave, is zero and has nothing to rotate, so the line's length stops mattering entirely . That is the only condition under which a cable behaves like the ideal wire of first-year circuits."},{"h":"Watching it rotate","i":"explore","x":"Set a load and walk back along the line. The left panel is the plane from chapter 06; the right is resolved into resistance and reactance as you move. Three things to notice while sweeping. The dot travels clockwise and returns to its start after , which is why the traces on the right are periodic with that period, not . The resistance and reactance both swing wildly even though the load is fixed - a 25 resistor seen through a line can look like almost anything. And the trace crosses the real axis twice per half wavelength: those two crossings are the voltage minimum and maximum of chapter 07."},{"h":"Why the minimum trick works","i":"minmax","x":"Chapter 07 offered a check without proof: at a voltage minimum the impedance looking toward the load is purely real and equals . That falls out immediately now. A voltage minimum is where points along the negative real axis , so . Substituting into the boxed expression: And at a maximum , giving the mirror result: So the standing-wave extrema are not merely places where the envelope peaks - they are the two points per half wavelength where the line looks like a pure resistance . That is why slotted-line measurements target them, and it is the fact every stub-matching procedure in chapter 11 is built on."},{"h":"Folding the line into a circuit","i":"circuit","x":"The practical payoff of is that it lets you delete the line . A generator of open-circuit voltage and internal impedance , driving a length of line into a load, becomes an ordinary two-element divider: That gives conditions at the input. To get back to the wave amplitudes - and hence to conditions anywhere along the line - evaluate the general solution at and solve for : With known, every quantity in the problem is determined: the voltage and current at any point, the power delivered, the standing-wave pattern. The full recipe, in order: Step 2 is the only one that involves transmission-line theory at all. Steps "}]},
  {"p":"line-lengths.html","t":"Line lengths and transformers","n":"09","l":"Two particular lengths do something useful enough to have names. A half wavelength changes nothing at all; a quarter wavelength turns an impedance upside down. From that second fact comes the simplest matching network there is, and from stubs of controlled len","s":[{"h":"The half-wave repeat","i":"half","x":"Put into the tangent formula. Then , and , so both tangent terms vanish: And since the same argument applies to any whole number of half wavelengths, adding to a line changes nothing . This is the same fact as the rotation completing a full lap, seen from the impedance side. It has a practical edge worth passing on. A half-wave line repeats the load at the design frequency only . Move off that frequency and the electrical length is no longer exactly , so the repeat fails - and it fails faster the more half-waves you stacked up. A long cable is a narrowband component even when it looks like a plain connection."},{"h":"The quarter-wave inverter","i":"quarter","x":"Now put , so and . Divide numerator and denominator by the tangent before taking the limit and the finite terms drop out: In normalised form, where , it is cleaner still: A quarter-wave line turns a normalised impedance into its reciprocal - which is to say, it turns impedance into admittance . That single sentence is why the Smith chart has an admittance overlay, and why it is the same chart rotated half a turn. Chapter 10 makes that explicit. The first row is the one that shows up in real hardware. A shorted quarter-wave stub presents an open circuit at the design frequency but a dead short at DC - which is exa"},{"h":"The quarter-wave transformer","i":"transformer","x":"Turn the inverter around and it becomes a matching network. Suppose a real load must be matched to a line of impedance . Insert a quarter-wave section of some other impedance between them. Looking into that section: For a match we need , which fixes immediately: That is the whole design. One section, one formula, no iteration - and it is exact at the design frequency. The figure shows the catch. is exactly zero at and climbs on both sides, because away from the design frequency the section is no longer a quarter wavelength. Bandwidth is not a property of the transformer - it is a property of the transformer plus "},{"h":"Stubs: reactance to order","i":"stubs","x":"Chapter 08 gave the input impedance of a shorted and an open line. Both are purely imaginary , and both sweep every value as the length changes: That makes a length of line a tunable reactive component . Need a particular inductance at 5 GHz? Cut a shorted stub to the length whose tangent gives it. At microwave frequencies this is often easier, cheaper and far more repeatable than sourcing a lumped part - and it is why boards at those frequencies are covered in little rectangular stubs. Read down the two columns and the pattern is clear: the open stub is the shorted stub shifted by a quarter wavelength , which is"}]},
  {"p":"matching.html","t":"Impedance matching","n":"11","l":"Every chapter so far has been describing what a mismatched line does. This one undoes it. Two tools - a length of line, and a stub - are enough to turn any passive load into a perfect match, and the Smith chart turns the design into a two-step construction.","s":[{"h":"What matching buys","i":"why","x":"The goal is stated in one line: make the impedance seen at the input equal , so that and no wave returns. Four things follow from achieving it. The second row is the one that changes how a system is built. An unmatched line is a component whose behaviour depends on its own length, so every cable becomes part of the design. Match it and the cable is just a cable again."},{"h":"When the load is real: the quarter-wave transformer","i":"quarter","x":"Chapter 09 already solved this case. A real load matches to through a quarter-wave section of , and that is the whole design. A complex load can be brought into that case first. Chapter 08 showed that at a voltage minimum the impedance is purely real and equals . So: walk along the line to the first minimum, and transform from there. It works, and for a fixed frequency it is clean. The drawback is practical: comes out as whatever the algebra demands, and on microstrip that means a track of a particular width you may not want, or on coax a cable impedance nobody sells. Stub matching avoids that entirely , because "},{"h":"Single-stub matching","i":"stub","x":"The idea is two moves, and each move fixes one of the two numbers you need to fix. Two points of technique are worth stating plainly. We work in admittance because the stub goes in parallel, and parallel elements add admittances - that is the only reason chapter 10 bothered with the overlay. And the stub is purely reactive , so it can change without touching ; it cannot fix the real part, which is why the travel has to come first. Normalised admittance splits as , where is the conductance and the susceptance - the admittance counterparts of and . The circle is simply the set of all points whose conductance is 1, "},{"h":"Below microwave: the L-network","i":"lumped","x":"Stubs need a length of line comparable with a wavelength. At 100 MHz that is a couple of metres, which no circuit board will carry, so lower frequencies use lumped matching instead: two reactive components in an L. Matching is then a path from the load to the centre using only those two kinds of arc. Two elements are always enough, because one constant- circle and one constant- circle through the centre will always intersect - usually twice, which is again why there are two solutions. The trade-off against stubs is worth naming. Lumped parts are tiny at low frequency and hopeless at high frequency, where their ow"},{"h":"Nothing matches over all frequencies","i":"bandwidth","x":"Every network here is designed at one frequency. Move away from it and the electrical lengths change, the reactances change, and the match degrades - exactly as chapter 09's transformer did. This is not a limitation of any particular technique. There is a theoretical bound, the Bode-Fano limit , which says that for a given load the total match quality available across all frequencies is fixed: you may trade a deeper match over a narrow band against a shallower one over a wide band, but the budget cannot be enlarged. The engineering consequence is simple to state. If you need more bandwidth, use more sections - tw"},{"h":"Where this leaves you","i":"end","x":"The chain is complete. A changing electric field makes a magnetic one, which makes an electric one a little further along, and the disturbance walks. Confine it between two conductors and the same physics reads as a ladder of and . Terminate that ladder in the wrong impedance and a wave comes back. Two waves interfere into a standing pattern; that pattern makes the impedance depend on where you stand; and that dependence, drawn on a disc, is the chart you just used to cancel the whole thing out. Every step was derived rather than asserted, and nothing was used before it was earned. The factors of two that looked "}]},
  {"p":"microstrip.html","t":"Microstrip and real lines","n":"05","l":"So far the four line parameters have been given numbers. They are not free: every one is fixed by the cross-section and the materials. This chapter computes them for the geometries you actually build, and looks at where microstrip stops obeying the model it wa","s":[{"h":"Geometry sets everything","i":"geometry","x":"Chapter 04 closed with a result worth restating, because it organises this whole chapter. For any TEM line, So the cross-section cannot change the speed. Squeeze the conductors together and rises while falls in exact compensation. What the geometry does control is the ratio, and therefore : Each of the first three is a genuine TEM structure: the fields sit entirely in one uniform dielectric, so is unambiguous and the formulas are exact. A quick check on the coax expression - a 50 cable with a PTFE dielectric ( ) needs , so . That is the ratio you find in real RG-142."},{"h":"Why microstrip is different","i":"quasi","x":"Microstrip is a track on top of a substrate with a ground plane underneath. It is the dominant line in practice - it is simply what a circuit board is - and it breaks the assumption every formula above relies on. The field lines leaving the track do not all pass through the substrate. Some arc through the air above it. So the wave is travelling through two dielectrics at once, with different permittivities, and no single describes it. That has a real consequence: a pure TEM wave cannot exist on this structure. A true TEM mode needs the same phase velocity everywhere in the cross-section, and here the air region w"},{"h":"The Hammerstad formulas","i":"hammerstad","x":"Microstrip has no exact closed form, but accurate curve fits exist. These are Hammerstad's, accurate to about 1 % over the usual range, and they are what most tools use internally. Everything depends on the single ratio - track width over substrate thickness. Then the impedance, in two branches: The structure of both is worth reading even if you never evaluate them by hand. Wider track means lower impedance - more capacitance to the ground plane, less inductance. And sits under a square root in the denominator of each, so a higher-permittivity substrate lowers for the same geometry. Try the narrow and wide preset"},{"h":"Where the model stops","i":"limits","x":"Everything so far treats microstrip as an ideal quasi-TEM line with frequency-independent parameters. Four effects break that, and knowing when each one bites is the difference between a board that works and one that needs a second spin. Skin effect deserves a line of its own because it explains a fact every RF engineer meets. At 1 GHz in copper the skin depth is about 2 m - the current is confined to a film far thinner than the conductor. Making the track thicker buys almost nothing; making it wider , or smoother, is what reduces loss. It is also why in chapter 04 is not really a constant: it climbs as ."}]},
  {"p":"prologue-helmholtz.html","t":"The wave equation and Helmholtz","n":"","l":"Chapter 01 argued in words that the two curl laws feed each other. Here that argument is made algebraically: take the curl of one, substitute the other, and the coupled pair collapses into a single equation whose solutions travel at a speed fixed entirely by t","s":[{"h":"Decoupling the two curl equations","i":"decouple","x":"The obstacle is that and appear in both equations at once. Faraday gives in terms of ; Amp re gives in terms of . Neither can be solved alone. The trick is to take the curl a second time . Doing so on Faraday's law produces a term in , which Amp re's law lets you replace - and vanishes from the problem entirely. In a source-free region ( , ) the result is: Compare it against the standard form of a wave equation, , and the propagation speed reads off immediately:"},{"h":"Dropping to the phasor domain","i":"phasor","x":"The wave equation still carries two derivatives in time. Almost everything in RF is time-harmonic - driven at a single frequency - and for that case the time dependence can be removed entirely. Write each field as a complex amplitude carrying an factor that we agree to keep implicit: Every time derivative now becomes a multiplication, because : Substituting into the wave equation turns a partial differential equation in four variables into one in three: This is the single most useful move in the subject, and it is worth being clear about what it costs. You give up the ability to describe transients - switching a "},{"h":"The simplest solution","i":"plane","x":"Take a wave travelling in with its electric field along . Helmholtz reduces to an ordinary differential equation in one variable: Two exponentials: one decaying in , one in . A forward wave and a backward wave. Every result in the rest of this site is a consequence of those two terms and what happens where they meet a boundary. In the lossless case , and restoring the time factor turns the forward term into A point of constant phase satisfies , so it moves at - giving the phase velocity and the wavelength . Two features of that picture are worth naming, because both carry into transmission lines unchanged. The fi"}]},
  {"p":"prologue-maxwell.html","t":"Maxwell's equations","n":"","l":"Four statements about how electric and magnetic fields relate to charge, to current, and to each other. Three were known before Maxwell. The fourth he corrected, and that correction is the reason a field can leave its source and never come back.","s":[{"h":"The four laws","i":"four","x":"In differential form, for fields in a medium described by permittivity and permeability : Read them as physical claims rather than as symbols: The word doing the work in the last two is curl . A curl is a circulation: it says the field forms closed loops around whatever sits on the right-hand side. So Faraday's law says a changing is encircled by , and Amp re's law says a changing is encircled by ."},{"h":"The term Maxwell added","i":"dcurrent","x":"Before Maxwell, Amp re's law read simply : magnetic fields encircle currents. That version is not merely incomplete, it is self-contradictory , and the contradiction shows up in the most ordinary circuit imaginable. Charge a capacitor. Amp re's law in integral form says the magnetic field around a closed loop equals the current passing through any surface bounded by that loop. Take the loop around the wire. Stretch one surface flat across it - the wire punches through, so the enclosed current is . Now stretch a different surface, bulging out so it passes between the capacitor plates. Same loop, same boundary, but"},{"h":"Why this makes a wave","i":"cycle","x":"Trace the loop. An electric field somewhere starts changing. By Amp re-Maxwell, that change wraps a magnetic field around it. But that magnetic field is itself now changing in time - and by Faraday, a changing magnetic field wraps an electric field around itself , slightly further along. That new electric field is also changing, and the cycle repeats. Neither field is the cause and neither is the effect. They regenerate each other, and the regeneration happens a little further along each time, so the disturbance propagates . Nothing material moves; no charge is present. Once launched, the pattern is self-supporti"},{"h":"Integral and differential forms","i":"forms","x":"The same four laws are written two ways, and it is worth being fluent in both. The differential form states what happens at a point and is what you manipulate algebraically. The integral form states what happens over a region and is what you use when a problem has symmetry you can exploit."}]},
  {"p":"propagation.html","t":"Propagation on a line","n":"04","l":"Two coupled equations, decoupled the same way the prologue decoupled the field laws. Out come the two numbers that describe any transmission line completely: how a wave changes as it travels, and the fixed ratio of voltage to current while it does.","s":[{"h":"Decoupling, again","i":"decouple","x":"Chapter 03 ended with two equations, each giving one quantity's slope in terms of the other: where and . Neither can be solved alone, and the fix is the one from the prologue: differentiate one and substitute the other. Differentiating the first with respect to produces a , which the second replaces - and leaves the problem. A second-order equation of that form has exponential solutions, and there are two of them: Everything in the remaining chapters is a consequence of those two terms and what happens where they meet a boundary. There is no third solution: a uniform line supports exactly one wave in each directi"},{"h":"The propagation constant","i":"gamma","x":"is complex, and its two parts do separate jobs. Writing and expanding the forward term: And itself comes straight from the four line parameters: That resemblance is not decorative. The circuit model was built to describe the same physics the field equations describe, so the two expressions had to agree - and , mean exactly what they meant there. The attenuation unit deserves a note. A neper is a natural-log unit: Np/m means the amplitude falls by a factor every metre. Datasheets quote decibels instead, and the conversion is ."},{"h":"Characteristic impedance","i":"z0","x":"The wave equation gave the voltage. The current follows by putting that solution back into the telegrapher's equation it came from, and the result is remarkably tidy: One more caution. relates to for one wave . On a line carrying both waves, the total voltage divided by the total current is not - it varies with position, and working out what it is instead is the subject of chapter 08."},{"h":"The lossless line","i":"lossless","x":"Set and everything simplifies at once: The derived quantities follow immediately: That does not depend on frequency is worth pausing on. Every frequency component travels at the same speed, so a pulse made of many components keeps its shape. A lossy line generally does not have this property, and the resulting spreading - dispersion - is what limits data rates on long cables."},{"h":"Seeing the parameters move","i":"explore","x":"Everything above is one square root of a ratio of two complex numbers. Change the four line parameters and watch what happens to , and the wave itself. The window is a fixed metre of line , so it never rescales under you. Three things are worth doing deliberately: The two presets on the right are the ones worth understanding, because together they separate the two things and control. depends on their ratio; depends on their product. So you can move one while holding the other fixed: That second preset is the important one, and it is the reason is printed on the figure. Nothing about the drawn wave changes when yo"},{"h":"Why 50 is a high-frequency statement","i":"dispersion","x":"holds when and - that is, when the reactive terms dominate the lossy ones. At low frequency they do not, and the same cable behaves entirely differently. At the bottom of that sweep , a few hundred ohms and purely real - the line behaves as a resistive divider, not as a wave medium. Through the middle it is complex, with dipping toward -45 . Only above roughly a megahertz does it settle onto the flat, real 50 that the cable is sold as. So \"RG-58 is 50 \" is shorthand for \"well above its transition frequency, this cable's is 50 \". For the RF work in the rest of this site that is always true, and can be treated as a"}]},
  {"p":"reflection.html","t":"Reflection at a load","n":"06","l":"A line supports one wave in each direction. Until now the backward one has had no reason to exist. Put something on the end that the forward wave alone cannot satisfy, and it appears - not as a defect, but because the algebra has no other solution.","s":[{"h":"Why a wave comes back","i":"why","x":"Chapter 04 gave the general solution on a lossless line. Put the load at , with the line running off in the direction toward the generator: At the load, , both exponentials become 1: The load imposes one condition and one only: the ratio of voltage to current at that point must equal . Nothing else about the load matters. Now look at what that demands. Suppose - only a forward wave. Then the ratio is forced to be exactly, whatever the load happens to be. So unless , a single wave cannot satisfy the boundary condition , and the only remaining freedom is . It takes whatever value makes the equation true."},{"h":"The reflection coefficient","i":"gamma","x":"Solve that equation for the ratio of the two waves and the result is compact: Read the formula as a comparison. The numerator is how far the load is from the line ; the denominator normalises it. A load equal to gives zero. A load very different from gives something approaching unit magnitude. The last row is worth dwelling on. A capacitor or inductor cannot absorb average power , so it must send every watt back - hence , exactly as for a short or an open. What distinguishes them is only the phase, and that phase is what shifts the pattern along the line."},{"h":"\u0393 on the complex plane","i":"explore","x":"Because for any passive load, every possible lives inside the unit circle. That disc is the natural home of this subject, and it is where the Smith chart will be built. Move the load around and note which regions of the disc it reaches. Purely resistive loads land on the real axis : those greater than to the right of the origin, those smaller to the left. Purely reactive loads land on the unit circle itself - full reflection, phase set by how reactive. Everything else fills the interior."},{"h":"Power, and what reflection costs","i":"power","x":"Power in a travelling wave goes as amplitude squared, so the fraction reflected is and the fraction delivered is whatever is left: Engineers usually quote this logarithmically, as return loss : how far below the incident wave the reflected one sits. Note that a larger return loss in dB means a better match, which reads backwards until you remember it is a loss suffered by the reflected wave. A 20 dB return loss means the reflection is a hundredth of the incident power."},{"h":"Looking from further up the line","i":"moving","x":"was defined at the load. Stand a distance back toward the generator and the two waves have travelled different distances to reach you - the reflected one has gone out and come back, an extra . Chapter 02 showed why only that difference matters, and it gives: Three consequences follow immediately, and all three shape everything after this chapter. On a lossy line the picture changes in one way only: carries a real part too, so the magnitude shrinks as you move back. The locus spirals inward instead of circling. A long enough lossy line looks matched from the input regardless of what is on the end - which is true, "}]},
  {"p":"smith-chart.html","t":"The Smith chart","n":"10","l":"Not a nomogram, not a lookup table, and not a relic of the era before calculators. It is the impedance plane redrawn on the disc - and once you see which curves are circles and why, the whole thing becomes obvious rather than intimidating.","s":[{"h":"The problem it solves","i":"why","x":"Everything so far has moved between two descriptions of the same thing. Impedance is what components have; is what waves do. The map between them is the M bius pair from chapter 06, in normalised form : Each description makes different things easy, and that is the whole difficulty. Smith's idea was to stop choosing. Draw the impedance grid onto the plane , and both become easy at once: travel along the line is still a plain rotation, and you can read the impedance directly off the grid you are standing on."},{"h":"Why the grid is made of circles","i":"circles","x":"Write and , and push the second formula through. Separating real and imaginary parts gives Now ask: for a fixed , what curve do and trace? Clear the denominator, collect terms and complete the square, and a circle appears - exactly, not approximately. Both families are drawn below, one at a time, so you can see the chart assemble itself."},{"h":"The chart itself","i":"chart","x":"Drag anywhere inside the circle to set a load. The dashed circle through your point is the constant-SWR circle : the path the impedance follows as you move along the line, because never changes on a lossless line. Sweep the slider and watch three facts from earlier chapters become one motion. The point travels clockwise ; it returns to the start after half a wavelength ; and it stays on a circle of constant radius , because a lossless line cannot change . Where that circle crosses the real axis on the left you are at a voltage minimum and ; where it crosses on the right you are at a maximum and ."},{"h":"The admittance overlay","i":"admittance","x":"Matching networks add components in parallel as often as in series, and parallel elements add admittances, not impedances. Working in ought to need a second chart. It does not. Chapter 09 showed that a quarter-wave line inverts a normalised impedance. In terms a quarter wave is a half rotation, so: That minus sign has to be applied somewhere, and there are two places to put it. Both are in common use, and they are the same statement written two ways. Nothing physical moves in either case. is one number, fixed by the load and by where you are standing on the line. What changes is the bookkeeping: in the first vers"},{"h":"What you can read off it","i":"using","x":"A worked case, all of it read rather than computed. Take on a 50 line, so - the fourth preset above. Every one of those is checkable against the figure above: load the fourth preset, and the readouts show and at the load. Sweep to and the point lands on the negative real axis with . Four numbers, no algebra. That is the argument for the chart, and it is why it survived the calculator: it is not faster arithmetic, it is a picture of the whole solution space at once , in which you can see where a match is and how to get there. The chart above is deliberately stripped down, so that the geometry stays visible. For a "}]},
  {"p":"smith-tool.html","t":"Smith chart workbench","n":"","l":"Both grids, the scales round the rim, and the radially scaled parameters underneath. Enter a load to read it off, or let the solver design the match and draw the construction as the arcs you would draw by hand.","s":[{"h":"Reading the outer scales","i":"reading","x":"The three rings around the chart are the part people skip, and they are the part that makes the chart usable without a calculator. From the inside out: Both wavelength rings run from to and then start again, because turns through a full every half wavelength. That is the same repeat chapter 09 derived, printed on the rim. To use them the way you would on paper: find your load, read its position on the toward generator ring, add the distance you are travelling, and read the new position back off the same ring. The difference between two readings is a distance; the readings themselves are arbitrary until you subtra"},{"h":"The scales under the chart","i":"nomograph","x":"Everything on the strip below the chart is a function of one number: , the distance from the centre. That is why a real chart prints them as a ruler rather than as curves - measure the radius of your point with a compass, lay it on the ruler, and read all six at once. The transmission coefficient is not on the ruler, and the reason is worth saying out loud: it depends on the angle of , not only its magnitude, so it is not a radial quantity at all. It is in the readout row instead, where it belongs."},{"h":"What the solver does","i":"methods","x":"Five methods. Each returns every solution rather than the first one found, and draws the circuit it produces underneath, with lengths in wavelengths and in millimetres at your frequency. Pozar's chapter on matching also covers binomial and Chebyshev multisection transformers and tapered lines. Those are not here, and not because they are hard: they are broadband designs built by choosing a set of section impedances from a polynomial, and there is nothing for the chart to construct. They belong with the algebra, not the geometry. Travel until the admittance is , then cancel with a stub in parallel. The constant-SW"},{"h":"A worked example, button by button","i":"walk","x":"Press example in the load panel. That sets on a line at 1 GHz, a load that is both too resistive-low and capacitive - awkward enough to be worth a construction. Then change the load and do it again. The whole point of the chart is that the construction is identical every time; only the arcs move."}]},
  {"p":"standing-waves.html","t":"Standing waves","n":"07","l":"A standing wave is not a kind of wave. It is the two travelling waves of the last chapter, caught in the act of adding up. Everything about minima, maxima and SWR follows from that, and from one observation: their relative phase depends on where you stand, nev","s":[{"h":"Two waves, one line","i":"sum","x":"Measure distance back from the load. The total phasor voltage is the sum of the two waves, and one factoring step does all the work: The first factor has magnitude one, so it contributes nothing measurable. What is left is the whole subject: Picture it in the complex plane: a fixed unit vector, and a second vector of length whose angle depends on where you stand. When they point the same way the lengths add; when they oppose, they subtract. Crucially, this depends only on , never on . The pattern is fixed in space. Each point on the line simply oscillates at its own fixed amplitude."},{"h":"Standing, travelling, and in between","i":"standing","x":"Every point on the line oscillates. What distinguishes the three cases is whether the pattern translates. The triangles mark the instantaneous zero crossings. In the first two panels they march; in the third they are pinned. Split the total into a travelling part of amplitude and a standing part of amplitude : the first carries the power that reaches the load, the second is what bounced. Only at does the travelling part vanish, and anything in between is a partially standing wave ."},{"h":"Why the pattern holds still","i":"why","x":"Stand at one fixed position and watch. Both waves oscillate at the same , so they are two phasors spinning together at the same rate. Their individual phases change constantly - that is what oscillating means - but their difference does not: So every position on the line carries a permanent verdict about how the two waves get along there, and that verdict is baked into the geometry. It cannot move. That is the whole mystery of why a standing wave stands. The factor of two in also hands you the quarter-wave rule for free. It is the round trip : the reflected wave covers each step out and back, so the relative phas"},{"h":"Standing wave ratio","i":"swr","x":"Nobody can measure directly. A ratio cancels it, which is why SWR is the number every lab reports: Note that carries only - the phase is thrown away. Two quite different loads can have identical SWR, which is exactly why a single SWR reading cannot identify a load, and why the position of a minimum has to be measured too."},{"h":"Reading a line backwards","i":"reading","x":"A detector cannot follow the carrier; it reads the envelope. That is enough, because the envelope's shape encodes completely. This figure is the one to keep in front of you. Maxima occur where the rotating vector has zero net phase, minima where it is exactly opposed: Which gives four steps that never change: A closed form for step 4 that avoids complex division under exam pressure: And a check worth teaching: at a voltage minimum the impedance looking toward the load is purely real and equals ; at a maximum it is . Any answer can be verified in seconds with that - and chapter 08 derives why."}]},
  {"p":"telegraphers.html","t":"The transmission line model","n":"03","l":"Kirchhoff's laws fail over a long wire but hold perfectly over a short one. So apply them to a piece short enough that they are exact, and let calculus assemble the pieces. Two coupled differential equations come out, and they govern every transmission line th","s":[{"h":"Four parameters per metre","i":"params","x":"Any transmission line is two conductors running alongside each other: coaxial cable, a twisted pair, a track over a ground plane. Whatever the geometry, the same four effects are present along the whole length. Every one is quoted per unit length , which is what the primes mean, and that convention is doing real work. A piece of line twice as long has twice the series resistance and twice the shunt capacitance, so a slice of length contains ohms, farads, and so on. The model then scales to a line of any length without being rebuilt. The two series elements govern what happens to the current flowing along the line"},{"h":"Kirchhoff on a slice","i":"derive","x":"Take one slice running from to . Because is small, the lumped approximation is valid inside it and the ordinary circuit laws apply. Around the loop. The voltage entering is and the voltage leaving is . The difference is whatever the series elements drop: At the node. The current entering is and the current leaving is . What went missing was diverted across the shunt elements: Divide both by and let it shrink to zero. Each left-hand side becomes a derivative by definition, and the telegrapher's equations appear: Look at what these two equations say. Each one relates a change along the line to a rate of change in t"},{"h":"In phasor form","i":"phasorform","x":"Chapter 01 established that for a single-frequency steady state, becomes multiplication by . Applying that removes time from the problem entirely and turns the partial derivatives in into ordinary ones: The two bracketed groups appear so often that they earn names. is the series impedance per unit length ; is the shunt admittance per unit length . In that shorthand the pair is simply Which is as compact as this subject gets. Two quantities, each one's slope set by the other. The next chapter decouples them the same way the prologue decoupled and - differentiate one and substitute the other - and out come the prop"},{"h":"The lossless case","i":"lossless","x":"Most of this site works with . Over the short lengths used at microwave frequencies, good cable really does lose very little, and every important structural result - reflection, standing waves, impedance transformation, the Smith chart - survives the simplification unchanged. Loss changes the numbers, not the shape of the theory. With the loss terms gone, the pair describes something purely conservative: energy shuttling between the magnetic field of the current and the electric field of the voltage, propagating without ever being dissipated. That is a wave, and chapter 04 confirms it by solving them."}]},
  {"p":"waves-phasors.html","t":"Waves, phasors and complex numbers","n":"01","l":"Everything that follows is written in one notation, and it is worth earning that notation properly rather than meeting it as a convention. A wave depends on two variables; a phasor removes one of them. What that costs, and what it buys, decides the shape of th","s":[{"h":"What makes something a wave","i":"wave","x":"A quantity that varies in time alone is an oscillation. A quantity that varies in space alone is a pattern. A wave is what you get when the two are locked together, so that the pattern in space shifts as time passes: The two terms inside the cosine carry different units and different meanings, and keeping them straight is most of the battle. is how far through the cycle you are . is how far along you are standing . Their combination is what travels. To see why, track a point of constant phase - a particular crest. Constant phase means does not change, so as advances, must advance with it: That is the phase veloci"},{"h":"Complex numbers, quickly","i":"complex","x":"Only one property of complex numbers actually matters here, and it is Euler's identity: Read geometrically, is a unit vector at angle in the complex plane. Multiplying by stretches it by and rotates it by . That single picture - a number as an arrow with a length and an angle - is why complex notation suits waves so well: a wave also has a size and a phase, and nothing else."},{"h":"The phasor","i":"phasor","x":"Here is the problem phasors solve. Write out a circuit equation for a sinusoid and you are differentiating cosines, which turn into sines, which turn into negative cosines. The algebra grows, and none of it is the physics - it is bookkeeping on a time dependence that is the same everywhere in the circuit. So factor it out. Every quantity in a time-harmonic problem - one driven at a single frequency - can be written as For the travelling wave at the top of this page, that means: The payoff is immediate. Because , differentiation in time stops being calculus and becomes multiplication: That substitution is what mak"},{"h":"Where the numbers land","i":"spectrum","x":"The same equations describe the entire electromagnetic spectrum; only the numbers change. What changes with them is which approximations survive. Two cautions on that table. Wavelength shortens inside a dielectric by , so a 12 cm free-space wave is nearer 6 cm on ordinary circuit board - which is why the numbers in chapter 05 come out smaller than intuition suggests. And nothing physical changes at any row of that table. There is no frequency at which circuit theory switches off. What changes is only the ratio of your hardware to , which is a continuous quantity, and the next chapter is about where in that contin"}]}
];
